Subject: Re: Small-batch cutover & rate-safety coordination for Brightline integration

Thanks — uploaded the basic scripts (see attached sdk-sample-curl-and-node.txt) and a tiny load driver. Quick implementation notes for Brightline engineers:

1) SDK setup (recommended minimal):
- Node: npm install @redwood/inference-sdk
- Python: pip install redwood-inference

2) Preferred request pattern (idempotent, short timeouts, exponential backoff with jitter):
- Client timeouts: 3s connect, 10s read for initial small-batch; increase read to 20s for longer-generation tests.
- Retry policy: up to 3 retries on 429/5xx with exponential backoff (base=300ms, factor=2) and randomized jitter +/-100ms.
- On 429 returned with "retry-after" header, respect header before attempting next request.

3) Sample Node snippet (trimmed):
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference-sdk');
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY, baseURL: 'https://api.redwood-internal.com/brightline/small-batch' });

(async () => {
  try {
    const res = await client.generate({ model: 'rwd-small-1', prompt: 'Summarize this order', max_tokens: 256 });
    console.log(res.text);
  } catch (err) {
    // exponential backoff and conditional retry handled by wrapper in sample driver
    console.error('gen error', err.message);
  }
})();

4) Endpoint migration note: when we flip traffic to the small-batch route you'll see client IPs shift to the dedicated gateway. The SDK supports an override header X-Redwood-Route which we will use during the test; you do not need to change client-side config except the API key.

5) Rate limit handling: the driver includes logic to parse 429 payloads that include per-route quota remaining. We'll surface that in logs (quota_remaining). If you prefer, we can emit a webhook to your ops channel in real time.

Let me know if you want the sample driver converted to Go or Java — can do quickly.

